      "The labor dispute delayed Opening Day and shortened the schedule to 144 games. Once play began, the Mets looked young, uneven, and more interesting than the records of the previous years. Rico Brogna became a fan favorite at first, Todd Hundley continued developing power, and Bobby Jones offered steadiness in the rotation.",
      "Dallas Green trusted several young players, including Edgardo Alfonzo, who began the season without a fixed position but kept earning at-bats. The club finished only 69–75, yet the National League East standings were distorted by Atlanta’s dominance and the short schedule. The Mets were still far from the Braves, but no longer felt built entirely from mistakes.",
      "The season’s importance sits in the players beginning to form the next competitive group. Alfonzo, Hundley, Jones, and Brogna gave the organization useful pieces, while the front office moved further from the failed early-1990s plan. There was no dramatic turning point. There was something the Mets badly needed: a normal year in which young players improved.",
