      "The Mets returned with a roster designed to finish the job. Piazza remained the star, Alfonzo supplied complete play, Leiter led the rotation, and newcomers Mike Hampton, Todd Zeile, and Derek Bell filled major roles. The club did not catch Atlanta, but 94 wins comfortably earned the Wild Card and brought another October chance.",
      "Bobby Jones threw a one-hit shutout to finish San Francisco in the Division Series. Hampton then dominated St. Louis, winning twice and closing the NLCS with a complete-game shutout. The Mets had their first pennant since 1986 and the first Subway World Series since the Dodgers and Yankees met in 1956.",
      "The World Series turned on missed chances. The Mets let a late lead escape in the 12-inning opener, Piazza’s broken bat led to the famous Roger Clemens incident in Game 2, and the Yankees kept winning close games. The series ended in five, but the result hides how narrow it felt. Losing to the Yankees made the pain sharper; winning the pennant still marked the full achievement of the Piazza-Alfonzo era.",
